<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 217 0 R/Pg 62 0 R/A[713 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

617 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 217 0 R/E(Communities Served:  FocusCounter is designed for students and workers who find motivation through gamification, measurable progress, and immediate feedback. It may particularly appeal to individuals who enjoy challenges and reward-based systems.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

618 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 217 0 R/E(Communities Left Out: The solution may disadvantage people whose responsibilities require frequent interruptions, including caregivers and workers who need to remain constantly reachable. It may also be less suitable for individuals who experience productivity-related anxiety, attention-regulation difficulties, or discomfort with competitive reward systems.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

619 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 217 0 R/E(Ethical Implications: The most relevant Ethical Design Principles are persuasion, usability, focus, and well-being.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

620 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 217 0 R/E(Although FocusCounter attempts to encourage positive behavior, its point-deduction mechanism could unintentionally promote guilt or anxiety. Users might begin to associate checking notifications with failure, even when interruptions are necessary or beneficial.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

621 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 217 0 R/E(Additionally, we must consider whether the system actually supports concentration or simply replaces one source of distraction with another. Users could become more concerned with maintaining their scores than completing meaningful work.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

622 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 217 0 R/E(From an ethical persuasion perspective, we should question whether penalizing behavior is an appropriate way to encourage focus. Instead, we could explore positive reinforcement, customizable notification exceptions, and ways to recognize effort without punishing necessary breaks.)/Pg 62 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

623 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 217 0 R/T(Ethical Implications - FocusCounter)/E(Ethical Implications - FocusCounter)/Pg 62 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

624 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 218 0 R/Pg 63 0 R/A[713 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

625 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(Communities Served: FocusToDos is designed for students, collaborative teams, and individuals who benefit from social accountability and encouragement. It may be particularly helpful for people who feel isolated when working independently and find motivation through supportive relationships.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

626 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(Communities Left Out: This solution may exclude people without established social support networks or friends who use the platform. It may also be less accessible to individuals who prefer privacy, experience social anxiety, or feel uncomfortable sharing their productivity with others.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

627 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(Ethical Implications: The most relevant Ethical Design Principles are privacy, persuasion, accessibility, and user involvement.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

628 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(Although FocusToDos aims to create a supportive environment, making individual progress visible to others introduces risks of social comparison, peer pressure, and unwanted surveillance.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

629 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(Users might feel obligated to demonstrate productivity to their friends, transforming encouragement into an additional source of stress. Moreover, shared to-do lists could reveal sensitive information about users' schedules, responsibilities, or personal challenges.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

630 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(An important ethical consideration is ensuring that social validation remains encouraging rather than manipulative. Users should be able to decide which tasks are visible, who can access their progress, and whether they want to receive encouragement.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

631 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 218 0 R/E(Moving forward, we should involve users with different social preferences and support networks in our design process to understand when accountability feels motivating and when it becomes uncomfortable.)/Pg 63 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

632 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 218 0 R/T(Ethical Implications - FocusToDos)/E(Ethical Implications - FocusToDos)/Pg 63 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

